    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d389">

                <pb facs="0396" n="384" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg384"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860I.00/432: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Estonia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Leonard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tallinn</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-07-05T12:00:00+02:00">July 5,
                            1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received July 5—11:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>82. I have been informed confidentially that the visit of the Soviet official
                    mentioned in my telegram No. 80<anchor n="59" xml:id="d389-f1"/> is in
                    connection with the (1) extension of coast and island defenses and
                    reorganization of the Estonian Army, (2) elections to be held for Parliament and
                    (3) possible change in the person of the President of Estonia, probably after
                    the elections.</p>
                <p>Dates of elections not yet announced but will probably be held soon, after which
                    radical political changes may be expected.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Leonard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d389-f1" n="59" xml:id="d389-f1-content">
                <p>Dated July 2, 5 p.m., not printed; it advised of the second visit of Andrey
                    Alexandrovich Zhdanov to Tallinn, where his presence caused uneasiness (740.0011
                    E. W. 1939/4356).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
